{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Changing the Rotary Pump Oil}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Changing_the_Rotary_Pump_Oil}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Changing the Rotary Pump Oil
\par \pard \plain\fs24 It is recommended that the rotary pump oil is changed at 6-monthly intervals, with intermediate checks on the oil level.
\par To change the oil:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f4 \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\f2 \tab Select CHAMBER vacuum vent and wait for the rotary pump to stop.
\par \f4 \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\f2 \tab Place a container of not less than 2.5 litres capacity under the oil drain plug at the bottom of the pump.  Remove the plug and drain the oil.
\par \f4 \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\f2 \tab Replace the drain plug and remove the filler plug at the top of the pump.  Refill the pump with the oil recommended in the pump manufacturers handbook.
\par \f4 \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\f2 \tab Replace the filler plug.  Refit the 'O' ring carrier, 'O' ring, pump hose manifold clamp ring and 2 screws.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f4 \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\f2 \tab While pumping the system down, check the condition of the oil mist filter (if fitted).  If a strong smell of oil vapour can be detected the elements of the filter must be changed.  In addition if the oil within the filter reaches the maximum mark, it should be drained.  The pump should be switched off for both of these operations.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Column Servicing}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Column_Servicing}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Cleaning Recommendations}
\b\fs32\cf1 Column Servicing
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The periods between cleaning will depend on the frequency of use, type of specimens and environmental conditions, etc.  As a general rule, if the required performance can be achieved then leave well alone.  Cleaning is only necessary if the resolution deteriorates and cannot be improved by adjustment.  The degree of cleaning needed can only be determined by inspecting the column components.
\par Routine cleaning consists of cleaning the grid and anode and inserting clean apertures.  If this does not restore the performance, then the whole column must be dismantled and cleaned.  The extent of this depends on the severity of contamination, which can only be found by inspecting the column as it is dismantled.
\par \pard 
\par \uldb \b Cleaning recommendations\plain\b\fs24 {\v Cleaning_recommendations}
\par \uldb Routine Column Cleaning\plain\b\fs24 {\v Routine_Column_Cleaning}
\par \uldb Cleaning the Aperture Assembly\plain\b\fs24 {\v Cleaning_the_Aperture_Assembly}  
\par \plain\fs24 
\par \b 
