{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Cleaning the Polepiece Assemblies}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Cleaning_the_Polepiece_Assemblies}
\pard \f2\b\fs32\cf1 Cleaning the Polepiece Assemblies
\par \plain\fs24 This is part of the\uldb  Basic Colummn Dismantling, Cleaning and Reassembly Procedure\plain\fs24 {\v Basic_Column_Dismantling__Cleaning_and_Reassembly}
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 1.\tab Carefully remove the spray apertures (3) from the bores of the polepiece assemblies noting their orientation.  These must be pulled out evenly so as not to distort them. 
\par 2.\tab Clean and wash the spray apertures. Inspect them for damage, replace if necessary.
\par 3.\tab Carefully clean the polepiece assemblies using lapping compound
\par 4.\tab The bores can be cleaned using cocktail sticks and the polepiece faces can be cleaned through the large pumping holes in the central copper spacer.
\par \pard\tx355 \fs32\cf2 The \b minimum\plain\fs32\cf2  amount of lapping compound must be used when cleaning the polepieces.\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \fs32\cf2 5.\plain\fs24 \tab All traces of lapping compound must be removed from each polepiece and they should then be washed several times in a \uldb recommended solvent\plain\fs24 {\v Cleaning_recommendations}.\fs32\cf2 
\par \plain\fs24 6.\fs32\cf2 \tab \plain\fs24 Clean and inspect the spray apertures.  If they are badly scratched or distorted replace them.
\par 
\par 
\par See \uldb \b Column Reassembly\plain\b\fs24 {\v Column_Reassembly}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Column Reassembly}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Column_Reassembly}
\pard \b\fs32\cf1 Column Reassembly
\par \plain\fs24 This is part of the\uldb  Basic Colummn Dismantling, Cleaning and Reassembly Procedure\plain\fs24 {\v Basic_Column_Dismantling__Cleaning_and_Reassembly}
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 1.\tab Refit the Spray Apertures into the Polepiece Assemblies.
\par 2.\tab Refit the C2 Polepiece assembly into the column boer using the special tool, ensure that the orientation is correct.
\par 3.\tab Place the wavy washer onto the top of the C2 Polepiece.
\par 4.\tab Replace the C1 Polepiece assembly into the column using the special tool, ensure that the orientation is correct. Refit the \ul anode support\plain\fs24 {\v Anode_Support} and secure using the four screws. Replace the firing unit and the high or low KV anode as required.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 5.\tab Pump the system and then check system resolution performance. If the resolution of the SEM remains poor contact your local service agent.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Looking for Vacuum Leaks}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Looking_for_Vacuum_Leaks}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Looking for Vacuum Leaks
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The first requirement is a method of measuring the vacuum.  Some methods, starting from the simplest, are:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 1.\tab Use the built in vacuum indicator, this may not be sensitive enough for leak hunting.
\par 2.\tab A better method is to disconnect the Penning gauge from the vac interlock PCB and connect to a commercially available Penning gauge box, e.g. a Penning 8 gauge box available from Edwards High Vacuum Ltd.
\par 3.\tab The best, and most expensive method is to fit a commercial leak detector system to the chamber, e.g. a mass spectrometer.  This only proves to be required in very rare cases.
\par \pard\tx355 The second thing you need is to find the leak.  This is done by putting some liquid or gas onto the leak.  The liquid or gas used must do two things.  It must quickly find its way through the leak and it must cause a reaction on the vacuum gauge.
\par \pard\tx355 If using a mass spectrometer leak detector the normal gas to use is helium.
\par \pard\tx355 To find a vacuum leak:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f4 \{bmc bm2.bmp\}\f2 \tab Set up the vacuum measuring equipment.
\par \f4 \{bmc bm2.bmp\}\f2 \tab Get some leak detecting fluid.
\par \f4 \{bmc bm2.bmp\}\f2 \tab Put a small amount of leak detecting fluid on a place in the vacuum system that is vacuum sealed.
\par \f4 \{bmc bm2.bmp\}\f2 \tab Look for some reaction on the vacuum gauge.  This reaction is normally an increase in pressure but it can sometimes be a decrease in pressure.  (If the fluid washes a piece of dust, or some 'O' ring grease, into the hole causing the leak it may seal it.)
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f4 \{bmc bm2.bmp\}\f2 \tab If no leak is found, test each vacuum seal in the system in turn.  Do this slowly as it may take the vacuum gauge several seconds to respond.
\par \f4 \{bmc bm2.bmp\}\f2 \tab If a leak is found, take the vacuum joint apart and renew the seal.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Anode Cleaning}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Anode_Cleaning}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Anode Cleaning
\par \pard \plain\fs24 This procedure is usually part of \uldb cleaning the Gun Assembly\plain\fs24 {\v Cleaning_the_Gun_Assembly}.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 1.\tab Vent the column and chamber to air.
\par 2.\tab Open the gun.
\par 3.\tab Lift out the anode (see \ul \b\ul Column\plain\b\fs24 {\v 19A.J.D}\plain\fs24 ) 
\par 4.\tab Close the gun and pump the system while the anode is cleaned. Take the anode to a clean area for cleaning.
\par 5.\tab Carefully clean all components using  the \uldb recommended cleaning method\plain\fs24 {\v Cleaning_recommendations}, ensuring that all traces of contamination and discolouration are removed.
\par 6.\tab Wash  in the recommended solvent in an ultrasonic cleaner.  Visually check that all traces of cleaning materials are removed.  Wash again in clean solvents.  Now wash again in more clean solvent.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 7.\tab Vent the column and chamber to air.
\par 8.\tab Open the gun.
\par 9.\tab Replace the anode into the anode support.
\par 10.\tab Pump system
\par \page
